1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:01,641
Welcome, trainer!

2
00:00:01,681 --> 00:00:04,834
This is your complete briefing for workshop
two,

3
00:00:04,874 --> 00:00:07,296
the advanced GitHub Copilot workshop.

4
00:00:07,536 --> 00:00:10,250
In a few minutes you will know the story,

5
00:00:10,290 --> 00:00:13,608
the approach, exactly how to get everything
ready,

6
00:00:13,648 --> 00:00:18,243
what to check, and how to rescue the session
when things go sideways.

7
00:00:18,283 --> 00:00:19,855
Let's get you confident.

8
00:00:21,258 --> 00:00:25,211
Start with the story, because your attendees
will remember it.

9
00:00:25,251 --> 00:00:29,139
Sam is the support lead at Harbor and Pine,
a fictional shop.

10
00:00:29,179 --> 00:00:30,749
It is Monday, nine fifty.

11
00:00:30,789 --> 00:00:35,771
The ten o'clock meeting needs one answer:
what should the engineers fix first?

12
00:00:36,011 --> 00:00:39,666
That single question is the scenario for all
four labs.

13
00:00:41,092 --> 00:00:43,848
The sample app is the Issue Triage Board.

14
00:00:43,888 --> 00:00:48,212
It runs on Node twenty two or newer, with a
built-in web server,

15
00:00:48,252 --> 00:00:51,145
zero dependencies, and port three thousand.

16
00:00:51,185 --> 00:00:55,236
It holds eight fictional issues, one oh one
to one oh eight.

17
00:00:55,476 --> 00:00:57,830
Six are active, and two are closed.

18
00:00:57,870 --> 00:01:00,086
The workshop ships it unfinished,

19
00:01:00,126 --> 00:01:03,642
with four deliberate stubs: priority says
untriaged,

20
00:01:03,682 --> 00:01:07,471
search passes everything through, the
summary is locked,

21
00:01:07,511 --> 00:01:11,368
and export returns a five oh one with no
download button.

22
00:01:11,608 --> 00:01:14,662
Your attendees finish those four pieces.

23
00:01:16,092 --> 00:01:17,559
Here is the real goal.

24
00:01:17,599 --> 00:01:21,395
Attendees should leave understanding why,
not just what.

25
00:01:21,435 --> 00:01:24,889
Every lab step follows the same pattern:
what, why,

26
00:01:24,929 --> 00:01:28,383
how, what you should see, what if not, and
reflect.

27
00:01:28,623 --> 00:01:34,763
If you remember one thing to say out loud,
it is this: the why is the lesson.

28
00:01:36,175 --> 00:01:39,168
Five ideas trip people up, so teach them
early.

29
00:01:39,208 --> 00:01:43,039
First, subagents give context isolation, not
file isolation:

30
00:01:43,079 --> 00:01:47,492
a subagent has a fresh context, but it can
still edit the same files.

31
00:01:47,732 --> 00:01:50,156
Second, skills grant no tools:

32
00:01:50,196 --> 00:01:54,754
a skill is a recipe card, and tools come
from the agent.

33
00:01:54,794 --> 00:01:59,599
Third, a worktree isolates files, but not
ports or secrets.

34
00:01:59,839 --> 00:02:02,814
Fourth, ownership and leases are advisory:

35
00:02:02,854 --> 00:02:06,332
they work because everyone agrees to follow
them.

36
00:02:06,372 --> 00:02:12,003
Fifth, a local handoff is not delegation:
two separate chats are not subagents.

37
00:02:13,425 --> 00:02:15,569
The approach is a small team.

38
00:02:15,609 --> 00:02:18,129
A coordinator plans and delegates.

39
00:02:18,169 --> 00:02:22,120
A read-only reviewer checks the work and
cannot edit.

40
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:26,000
In lab three there are two workers, search
and summary,

41
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:30,082
and one integrator who merges the results
and runs the gates.

42
00:02:30,122 --> 00:02:33,963
The default route is a single writer, which
is the safest.

43
00:02:34,203 --> 00:02:36,768
The parallel route is the stretch goal,

44
00:02:36,808 --> 00:02:40,777
and it only works with frozen contracts and
clear ownership.

45
00:02:42,175 --> 00:02:43,808
Why bother with ownership?

46
00:02:43,848 --> 00:02:47,733
Because when two helpers edit the same file
at the same time,

47
00:02:47,773 --> 00:02:49,663
one silently erases the other.

48
00:02:49,703 --> 00:02:53,781
Nobody notices until the tests fail, or
worse, until they don't.

49
00:02:55,175 --> 00:02:57,429
The cure is layers of protection.

50
00:02:57,469 --> 00:02:58,611
Layer one: lanes.

51
00:02:58,651 --> 00:03:00,627
Each worker owns named files,

52
00:03:00,667 --> 00:03:05,563
like the search file or the summary file,
and nobody else touches them.

53
00:03:05,803 --> 00:03:07,658
Layer two: frozen contracts,

54
00:03:07,698 --> 00:03:11,785
which are written agreements about function
names and shapes,

55
00:03:11,825 --> 00:03:15,169
so workers can build in parallel without
guessing.

56
00:03:15,209 --> 00:03:20,040
Layer three: one integrator, the only one
who touches the shared wiring.

57
00:03:20,281 --> 00:03:24,403
Layer four: worktrees, separate checkouts
for each worker.

58
00:03:24,443 --> 00:03:28,421
And remember the caveat: worktrees do not
isolate ports,

59
00:03:28,461 --> 00:03:32,870
secrets, or shared services, so give each
server its own port.

60
00:03:33,110 --> 00:03:38,364
When all else fails, drop to the safe route:
one writer at a time.

61
00:03:39,758 --> 00:03:43,813
Learn the scoring rule so you can check
answers quickly.

62
00:03:43,853 --> 00:03:47,104
Issue one oh one is high impact, worth
sixty.

63
00:03:47,144 --> 00:03:49,371
It is blocked, add twenty five.

64
00:03:49,411 --> 00:03:51,784
A hundred or more users, add ten.

65
00:03:51,824 --> 00:03:53,905
A week old or more, add five.

66
00:03:54,145 --> 00:03:58,500
That makes one hundred, and priority one
starts at eighty.

67
00:03:58,540 --> 00:04:00,697
Priority two starts at forty.

68
00:04:00,737 --> 00:04:03,273
Everything else is priority three.

69
00:04:03,313 --> 00:04:05,546
Closed issues are always zero.

70
00:04:06,967 --> 00:04:09,162
Here is the full answer key.

71
00:04:09,202 --> 00:04:12,675
Issue one oh one: one hundred, priority one.

72
00:04:12,715 --> 00:04:15,629
One oh two: fifty five, priority two.

73
00:04:15,669 --> 00:04:18,663
One oh three: fifteen, priority three.

74
00:04:18,703 --> 00:04:22,814
One oh four: forty, priority two, right on
the line.

75
00:04:23,054 --> 00:04:25,557
One oh five is closed, so zero.

76
00:04:25,597 --> 00:04:28,839
One oh six: thirty five, priority three.

77
00:04:28,879 --> 00:04:31,628
One oh seven: ten, priority three.

78
00:04:31,668 --> 00:04:34,581
And one oh eight is closed, so zero.

79
00:04:36,008 --> 00:04:38,262
And here is what done looks like.

80
00:04:38,302 --> 00:04:42,779
Searching payments returns two cards, one oh
one and one oh five.

81
00:04:42,819 --> 00:04:45,419
Searching keyboard returns one oh two.

82
00:04:45,459 --> 00:04:50,006
The summary shows eight total, six active,
and two active blocked.

83
00:04:50,246 --> 00:04:53,782
The export button says download evidence
JSON,

84
00:04:53,822 --> 00:04:57,203
and the file contains only issue one oh one.

85
00:04:58,633 --> 00:04:59,592
Plan the clock.

86
00:04:59,632 --> 00:05:01,722
The live session is three hours.

87
00:05:01,762 --> 00:05:06,383
Slides one to ten are the story and a demo,
the first fifteen minutes.

88
00:05:06,423 --> 00:05:09,978
Slides eleven to twenty three cover agents
and skills,

89
00:05:10,018 --> 00:05:12,774
with two demos, until thirty five minutes.

90
00:05:13,014 --> 00:05:17,141
Slides twenty four to thirty five cover
ownership and contracts,

91
00:05:17,181 --> 00:05:19,940
with a third demo, until the one hour mark.

92
00:05:19,980 --> 00:05:24,627
Then two hours of labs: thirty, thirty five,
forty, and fifteen minutes.

93
00:05:26,050 --> 00:05:27,981
Here is what each lab does.

94
00:05:28,021 --> 00:05:30,025
Lab one is the safe warm-up.

95
00:05:30,065 --> 00:05:31,996
Attendees write team rules,

96
00:05:32,036 --> 00:05:36,157
and build a read-only reviewer and a triage
policy skill.

97
00:05:36,197 --> 00:05:38,639
They do not change the app at all.

98
00:05:38,879 --> 00:05:42,412
Lab two builds the priority feature, with a
coordinator,

99
00:05:42,452 --> 00:05:46,687
read-only research, one builder, quality
gates, and a fresh review.

100
00:05:46,727 --> 00:05:50,323
Lab three is the big one: search and summary
in parallel.

101
00:05:50,363 --> 00:05:52,620
Route A, one writer, is the default.

102
00:05:52,860 --> 00:05:55,904
Route B uses two workers and frozen
contracts.

103
00:05:55,944 --> 00:05:58,184
Lab four adds the evidence export,

104
00:05:58,224 --> 00:06:01,804
runs the final gates, and ends with a
truthful report,

105
00:06:01,844 --> 00:06:06,431
where anything not run is marked not run,
then a short retrospective.

106
00:06:07,842 --> 00:06:11,225
Two days before class, send attendees the
preflight.

107
00:06:11,265 --> 00:06:16,162
It takes fifteen to twenty five minutes and
is not part of the three hours.

108
00:06:16,402 --> 00:06:20,286
They need Node twenty two or newer, Git, a
modern browser,

109
00:06:20,326 --> 00:06:24,549
VS Code with Copilot or the Copilot app, and
a Copilot account.

110
00:06:24,589 --> 00:06:28,135
Optionally they can read the story and
concepts docs,

111
00:06:28,175 --> 00:06:30,909
thirty five to forty five minutes in all.

112
00:06:31,149 --> 00:06:34,473
No cloud account, Azure, or MCP setup is
needed.

113
00:06:34,513 --> 00:06:40,150
But policies, quota, and rollouts can limit
agents, so ask people to check early.

114
00:06:41,550 --> 00:06:44,010
Give them the code one of two ways.

115
00:06:44,050 --> 00:06:48,510
Online: clone with the dash b flag, naming
the workshop branch.

116
00:06:48,750 --> 00:06:53,809
The remote main branch is empty, so a plain
clone gives an empty folder,

117
00:06:53,849 --> 00:06:56,712
and that is the number one setup mistake.

118
00:06:56,752 --> 00:06:59,616
Offline: use the sample app and labs zip.

119
00:06:59,856 --> 00:07:01,483
It has no git history,

120
00:07:01,523 --> 00:07:06,635
so run git init and make a baseline commit
before the worktree labs.

121
00:07:06,675 --> 00:07:08,606
And never run npm install.

122
00:07:08,646 --> 00:07:10,879
The app has zero dependencies.

123
00:07:12,300 --> 00:07:14,719
Before class, run four checks.

124
00:07:14,759 --> 00:07:18,326
Npm test should show fourteen passing tests.

125
00:07:18,366 --> 00:07:21,850
Npm run smoke should say HTTP smoke passed.

126
00:07:22,090 --> 00:07:24,775
Npm run check customizations should report

127
00:07:24,815 --> 00:07:27,499
zero active agents and zero active skills,

128
00:07:27,539 --> 00:07:30,548
which is correct, because lab one creates
them.

129
00:07:30,588 --> 00:07:35,868
And npm run verify solutions runs twenty six
tests against the instructor answers.

130
00:07:36,108 --> 00:07:40,384
Heads up: the acceptance checks, like npm
run acceptance priority,

131
00:07:40,424 --> 00:07:42,738
are expected to fail on the starter.

132
00:07:42,778 --> 00:07:43,915
That is by design.

133
00:07:45,342 --> 00:07:47,384
On the day, prepare the room.

134
00:07:47,424 --> 00:07:49,969
Start from a clean starter checkout.

135
00:07:50,009 --> 00:07:54,493
Check the board at a narrow width, and with
tab key navigation.

136
00:07:54,533 --> 00:07:58,442
Confirm that Copilot works and that quota is
available.

137
00:07:58,682 --> 00:08:03,678
To show the finished board, run npm run
start solutions on a different port,

138
00:08:03,718 --> 00:08:07,455
but never show an all-green board before
attendees start.

139
00:08:07,495 --> 00:08:09,708
Stop only the servers you started.

140
00:08:09,748 --> 00:08:12,888
And have your manual, one-writer fallback
ready.

141
00:08:14,300 --> 00:08:16,468
Check your surfaces and harnesses.

142
00:08:16,508 --> 00:08:20,106
Keep the Local profile and the Copilot
profile separate.

143
00:08:20,146 --> 00:08:23,483
New VS Code sessions default to the Copilot
harness,

144
00:08:23,523 --> 00:08:26,211
and Local needs an explicit fresh session.

145
00:08:26,451 --> 00:08:28,167
Check the agent's tool list,

146
00:08:28,207 --> 00:08:31,743
and check whether the Copilot app auto
creates worktrees.

147
00:08:31,783 --> 00:08:34,629
If the Agents window or subagents are
missing,

148
00:08:34,669 --> 00:08:37,327
fall back to ordinary chat with one writer.

149
00:08:37,567 --> 00:08:42,749
Never call two separate chats subagent
delegation, and never bypass restrictions.

150
00:08:44,175 --> 00:08:45,658
Troubleshooting time.

151
00:08:45,698 --> 00:08:48,705
Three first questions solve most problems.

152
00:08:48,745 --> 00:08:50,300
Did I restart the app?

153
00:08:50,340 --> 00:08:52,114
Am I in the right folder?

154
00:08:52,154 --> 00:08:53,999
And which harness am I on?

155
00:08:54,039 --> 00:08:57,481
Also, expected unfinished behavior is not a
bug.

156
00:08:57,721 --> 00:09:03,239
Untriaged badges and a five oh one on export
are the starter working as designed.

157
00:09:04,633 --> 00:09:06,630
Here are the common fixes.

158
00:09:06,670 --> 00:09:10,470
If the clone is empty, someone forgot the
dash b.

159
00:09:10,510 --> 00:09:16,189
If port three thousand is busy, set a
different port, then run npm start.

160
00:09:16,229 --> 00:09:19,480
If PowerShell blocks npm, use npm dot cmd.

161
00:09:19,720 --> 00:09:22,221
If an agent is missing from the picker,

162
00:09:22,261 --> 00:09:25,284
or approvals are blocked, use the manual
route.

163
00:09:25,324 --> 00:09:27,890
And if two sessions see different edits,

164
00:09:27,930 --> 00:09:30,953
remember worktrees: each one has its own
files.

165
00:09:32,383 --> 00:09:34,762
Watch the clock with the rescue table.

166
00:09:34,802 --> 00:09:38,325
At one hour seven minutes, if people are
stuck on rules,

167
00:09:38,365 --> 00:09:40,298
offer the two acceptable rules.

168
00:09:40,338 --> 00:09:45,071
At one hour nineteen, use the contract
policy, not invented business rules.

169
00:09:45,311 --> 00:09:49,349
At two oh five, default everyone to single
writer Route A.

170
00:09:49,389 --> 00:09:50,334
At two twenty,

171
00:09:50,374 --> 00:09:55,748
the separate feature installers can rescue
Route A and Route B independently.

172
00:09:55,788 --> 00:09:59,052
At two forty nine, apply the handoff if
needed.

173
00:09:59,292 --> 00:10:04,019
At two fifty three, any command you did not
run is marked not run.

174
00:10:04,059 --> 00:10:09,075
And at two fifty six, any unresolved failure
is reported as not ready.

175
00:10:10,467 --> 00:10:15,118
If agents collide, don't panic, and never
use a destructive reset.

176
00:10:15,158 --> 00:10:16,326
Follow six steps.

177
00:10:16,366 --> 00:10:17,534
Stop all writers.

178
00:10:17,574 --> 00:10:19,951
Preserve the diff as a patch file.

179
00:10:19,991 --> 00:10:21,443
Name the lease owner.

180
00:10:21,483 --> 00:10:23,007
Assign one integrator.

181
00:10:23,047 --> 00:10:24,215
Re-run the gates.

182
00:10:24,255 --> 00:10:26,276
And write down what happened.

183
00:10:26,516 --> 00:10:28,157
To undo workshop changes,

184
00:10:28,197 --> 00:10:32,124
every workshop command prints a backup ID
before it writes.

185
00:10:32,164 --> 00:10:35,351
Run npm run workshop restore with that exact
ID.

186
00:10:35,391 --> 00:10:39,788
Restore saves a rescue backup first, and a
partial ID is rejected.

187
00:10:41,217 --> 00:10:42,371
Finally, rehearse.

188
00:10:42,411 --> 00:10:47,150
Install all four answers on a practice copy,
check acceptance and tests,

189
00:10:47,190 --> 00:10:51,796
which should give eighteen passing tests,
then restore to the starter.

190
00:10:51,836 --> 00:10:54,915
At the end of lab four, ask four why
questions.

191
00:10:55,155 --> 00:10:59,021
Why did we freeze the contract before anyone
wrote code?

192
00:10:59,061 --> 00:11:01,183
Why was the reviewer read-only?

193
00:11:01,223 --> 00:11:04,252
Why is a passing test not the same as ready?

194
00:11:04,292 --> 00:11:07,112
And what would you not delegate, and why?

195
00:11:07,352 --> 00:11:10,705
If attendees can answer, they have the why.

196
00:11:12,133 --> 00:11:13,742
That's the whole briefing!

197
00:11:13,782 --> 00:11:18,687
You know the story, the team approach, the
setup, the checks, and the rescues.

198
00:11:18,727 --> 00:11:22,809
Go make your attendees' day, and remember:
the why is the lesson.

199
00:11:22,849 --> 00:11:24,457
Have a fantastic workshop!
